select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d16', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — equilibrium is a claim, not a coincidence", "markdown": "CPA writes ΣF = 0 when an object is at rest or moving at constant velocity. Honors treats that equation as a **claim you have to defend**: *I assert the forces sum to zero, here is the evidence, here is the reasoning.* A balanced free-body diagram is the beginning of an argument, not the end of one."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## What equilibrium actually asserts\n\nWhen $\\Sigma \\vec{F} = 0$, the acceleration is zero — *and that is the whole statement.* It does **not** say the object is at rest. A crate sliding across a floor at a steady 4 m/s is in equilibrium just as much as a crate sitting still: in both, velocity isn't changing, so the net force is zero.\n\nThis is where intuition fights you. The naive model says *'moving means there's a leftover force pushing it along.'* Equilibrium says the opposite: a thing can move forever at constant velocity with **zero** net force. The friction backward exactly cancels the push forward. To claim equilibrium, you must show **every component** sums to zero — not just the ones you happened to notice."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "warning", "title": "State your assumptions", "markdown": "An equilibrium claim is only as good as the model under it. Before you write ΣF = 0, name what you're assuming: that you've drawn **every** force (no hidden ones), that the object is a **point** (no rotation/torque to worry about), and that the surface, string, or support is **rigid** and not accelerating. If any of those breaks, your tidy ΣF = 0 breaks with it."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "This object IS / IS NOT in equilibrium. My evidence is ___. The assumption my claim depends on most is ___, and it would break if ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY: A sign hangs motionless from two angled cables. A classmate says 'it's not moving, so there are no forces on it.' Defend the correct equilibrium claim instead: explain what ΣF = 0 really asserts here, cite the forces that cancel, and name the one assumption your claim leans on hardest."}, {"id": "h5", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d16-equilibrium"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d17', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — choosing axes is a decision you defend", "markdown": "On an incline, CPA tilts the axes so x runs along the slope and resolves gravity into components. Honors does the same calculation — and then **justifies why that choice was the smart one.** Picking axes isn't a rule handed to you; it's a modeling decision, and a strong analyst can say out loud why it pays off."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Why tilt the axes?\n\nYou *could* keep x horizontal and y vertical. The physics would still be correct. But then **two** forces — the normal force and the slide — would each have x and y parts, and the motion would mix both axes. Messy.\n\nTilt the axes to match the slope and something clean happens: the object only accelerates **along** the incline, so the whole problem collapses onto one axis. The normal force points straight along the new y (no component to chase), and only **gravity** has to be decomposed:\n\n$$F_{\\parallel} = mg\\sin\\theta \\qquad F_{\\perp} = mg\\cos\\theta$$\n\nThe decomposition choice isn't about getting a different answer — it's about choosing the frame where the answer is **easiest to defend**. That is the honors move: not just *do* it, but be able to say *why this frame and not the other.*"}, {"id": "h3", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "warning", "title": "State your assumptions", "markdown": "Your incline result $F_\\parallel = mg\\sin\\theta$ quietly assumes several things: the ramp is **frictionless** (or you've added friction as its own term), the incline is **rigid** and doesn't deform, the angle $\\theta$ is measured from the **horizontal**, and the object is a **point** sliding without tipping or rolling. Name these before you trust the number — a stated assumption is a checkable one."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "I chose to tilt my axes along the slope because ___. With horizontal/vertical axes instead, the problem would be harder because ___. One assumption my decomposition rests on is ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY: For a block on a frictionless ramp, explain why you decomposed gravity along tilted (slope-aligned) axes rather than horizontal/vertical ones. Argue what specifically gets easier, and name one assumption your decomposition depends on."}, {"id": "h5", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d17-incline-components"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d18', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — one structure, not two units", "markdown": "Today the force tools come together. The honors move is to see that **kinematics and forces are not two separate subjects** — they're the front and back of a single machine. Force decides acceleration; acceleration decides how the motion unfolds. Connect the arc out loud."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Connect the arc — forces and kinematics are one structure\n\nTrace the chain you've built since Day 1:\n\n1. **Kinematics** gave you the language of *how* things move: position, velocity, acceleration — and the equations that link them.\n2. **Newton's laws** answered the *why*: $\\Sigma\\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ says the net force **sets** the acceleration.\n3. A **free-body diagram** is the bridge: it turns a physical situation into the one number — net force — that feeds $a = \\Sigma F / m$.\n\nSo the structure runs **one direction**: *forces → net force → acceleration → (back into kinematics) → the whole future motion.* Friction, the normal force, weight, tension — every contact force you've studied is just an input to that single pipeline. There was never a 'kinematics chapter' and a 'forces chapter.' There's one chain, and today you can finally see both ends of it at once. That is what it means to **own** Unit 1 rather than to have covered it."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "Forces connect to kinematics through ___. If I knew every force on the asteroid, the step that turns that into its future position is ___, because ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY: Explain, as a single connected chain, how a free-body diagram leads all the way to a prediction of an object's future position. Name each link (net force → acceleration → motion) and defend why you can't skip the middle step."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d18-forces-synthesis"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d19', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — rehearse the peer defense", "markdown": "On the transfer task you'll defend your reasoning **aloud** to a partner. Today is the dress rehearsal for that. While CPA closes practice gaps, honors also rehearses the AP1 move that the transfer task actually scores: defending a claim out loud, on the spot."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "tip", "title": "The claim–evidence–reasoning defense", "markdown": "An AP1 defense has three parts, in order:\n\n- **Claim** — the one sentence you're asserting (e.g. *'the sign is in equilibrium'*).\n- **Evidence** — the specific facts that support it (the forces, the numbers, the diagram).\n- **Reasoning** — the *why* that links evidence to claim, **including the assumptions** it rests on and where they'd break.\n\nMost answers stop at evidence. The honors bar lives in the **reasoning** step — that's the part a fast, correct student skips, and the part a partner will probe. Practice saying all three without notes."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d19-defend-aloud"}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "My weakest reasoning move is ___ (stating assumptions / linking evidence to claim / naming where a model breaks). To strengthen it before the transfer task I will ___.", "prompt": "PLAN: Of the three defense steps — claim, evidence, reasoning — which is your weakest when you have to do it ALOUD with no notes? Name the specific reasoning move you fumble most, and write a concrete plan to drill it before the transfer task."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d20', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — dress rehearsal for the transfer task", "markdown": "CPA runs one integrated analysis of 2026-XJ today. Honors runs that **and** rehearses the two moves the transfer-task rubric scores hardest: *stating your assumptions* and *locating this unit in the arc.* Treat today as the full-dress run-through, not just review."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Rehearse the two honors moves\n\nAs you walk through the asteroid analysis, narrate two things CPA doesn't have to:\n\n**1. State every assumption — and when it breaks.** Your constant-velocity prediction quietly assumes: velocity stays constant, no forces act over this leg, the asteroid is a point particle, and the motion stays in a plane. For each, say the condition that would break it. An unstated assumption is the most common way an honors '4' collapses to a '3'.\n\n**2. Locate Unit 1 in the year.** Be honest about the **edge** of what you've built. Unit 1's tools predict a *straight-line* position from constant velocity. They **cannot** tell you why the path will eventually **curve** (that's gravity, a later unit) or what an **impact** would do (that's momentum, later still). Naming that edge is the connect move — an analysis that pretends to answer everything is island-bound, and island answers cap Transfer at 3."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "One thing Unit 1 CANNOT yet answer about 2026-XJ is ___. The unit that will have to answer it is ___, because ___.", "prompt": "JUSTIFY: Name one specific question about 2026-XJ that Unit 1's physics genuinely CANNOT answer — and say which later unit will have to pick it up, and why our current tools fall short. Be precise about the edge of what you've built."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d20-locate-the-arc"}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d21', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors transfer task — same numbers, higher bar", "markdown": "You get the **same scenario and the same data** as CPA: 2026-XJ at 1.2×10⁸ km, 8,500 m/s at 15° toward Earth. The calculation isn't harder. What's raised is the **demand**: state your assumptions and their limits, defend the model in writing, and locate this task in the year's arc. The bar moves, not the numbers."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Part B — Justify (the honors raise)\n\nAfter the Part-A calculation (position, velocity components, uncertainty window, position-vs-time graph), you owe three things in writing:\n\n- **State every assumption** your prediction rests on — you have at least four (constant velocity, negligible force over this leg, point particle, motion confined to a plane) — and for **each**, the physical condition under which it would break.\n- **Defend the constant-velocity model** against the classmate who says *'acceleration is negligible, so it's a perfectly straight line we can pinpoint exactly.'* Their arithmetic is fine; the reasoning is not. Find the flaw.\n- **Translate the math into physics:** what the 15° angle means for whether it hits us, and why the uncertainty window *grows* over six months instead of staying fixed."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "Assumption: ___. It would break when ___.  (Repeat for each of your four.)", "prompt": "STATE EVERY ASSUMPTION AND WHEN IT BREAKS: List every assumption your six-month prediction rests on (at least four). For EACH one, pair it with the specific physical condition under which it would fail. A bare list of assumptions without breaking conditions caps your Reasoning at 3."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "exit_ticket", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "frame": "The classmate's MODEL error is ___. Their DATA error is ___. These are different because a model error comes from ___ while a data error comes from ___.", "prompt": "REBUTTAL — model error vs data error: Your classmate's numbers are right but their claim ('a perfectly straight line we can pinpoint exactly') is wrong for TWO separate reasons. Name both: the model error (the constant-velocity assumption) AND the data error (the ± measurement uncertainties). Explain why missing either one is an incomplete rebuttal."}] $honors$::jsonb);
select public.merge_honors_seed('u1-d22', $honors$ [{"id": "h1", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "note", "title": "Honors — the peer defense (90 seconds)", "markdown": "Today your reasoning gets tested by a partner, not a worksheet. **Protocol:** pair up. Each analyst defends their **assumptions list and rebuttal paragraph aloud for 90 seconds** while the partner does ONE job — hunt for an *unstated assumption* or an *unaddressed flaw.* Then swap. The defense, not the paper, is where it becomes clear who actually owns the reasoning. State the claim, give the evidence, walk the reasoning — and survive the probe."}, {"id": "h2", "type": "prose", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "markdown": "## Part C — Locate it in the year (the connect beat)\n\nClose with the **public briefing**: *what we know, what we don't, and what we cannot yet answer.* Name at least one thing Unit 1's physics **cannot** tell us about 2026-XJ — and which later question of the year has to. (Hint: what would bend that straight line?)\n\nA briefing that treats this prediction as the whole story is **island-bound** — it caps your Transfer score at 3, however polished it reads. Treat your prediction as *one link in a chain*: this is what we can say now, this is the edge of our reach, and this is who picks it up next."}, {"id": "h3", "type": "callout", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "variant": "tip", "title": "Why 4-3-4-3 is NOT 4-4-4-4", "markdown": "Your work is scored on **four independent dimensions**, letter grade set by the lowest:\n\n1. **Science** — is the physics correct? (and do your sig figs respect the ±uncertainty?)\n2. **Reasoning** — assumptions stated *with* breaking conditions; rebuttal catches *both* model error and data error.\n3. **Communication** — a peer could check your reasoning, not just your answer.\n4. **Transfer-to-asteroid** — you locate Unit 1 in the arc and name what it *can't* yet do.\n\nA fast, correct student who computes the position and writes a tidy paragraph lands at **4-3-4-3**: Science and Communication are fine, but Reasoning drops to 3 without assumptions-and-limits, and Transfer drops to 3 if the briefing is island-bound. The two 3s are not careless errors — they're the exact spots where speed substitutes for the honors moves. Closing them is what makes it 4-4-4-4."}, {"id": "h4", "type": "marzano", "visibilityTrack": "honors", "capture": true, "targetId": "u1.d22-honors-transfer"}] $honors$::jsonb);
